# MINIMUM NECESSARY CHANGE / ANTI-SPAGHETTI PRINCIPLE

Permanent. Not limited to the current phase. Build only what is necessary.

## Order of work

Before new code, inspect existing modules, utilities, components, packages, IPC, and tests.

```text
REUSE → EXTEND → LOCAL CHANGE → NEW ABSTRACTION
```

Never reverse this without a present-day reason. Phase plans must state:

- Existing code reused
- Existing code extended
- New code required

## Minimum necessary change

Smallest coherent change that fully satisfies the **approved** requirement.

Do not: architecture for hypothetical futures; generic frameworks for one use case; factories/strategies/providers when one implementation exists; wrapper layers around working APIs; one-use utility modules; interfaces with a single implementation unless testing/security/architecture requires the boundary; dead code; unused extension points; speculative config; placeholders for unapproved features.

## New abstraction gate

Every new production service, interface, adapter, package, shared utility, hook, context, domain layer, or IPC channel family needs:

- Why it must exist **now**
- Who uses it **now**
- Why extending the existing structure is worse
- What duplication or boundary it solves

“Future extensibility” alone is not enough.

A new package/module is justified only for a real boundary (independent domain, security/runtime, reusable engine, independently testable capability, dependency isolation). A new feature name is not a new package.

## Complexity warning (not a hard ban)

If a relatively small feature needs more than ~5 new production files, ~2 new abstraction layers, or a new service+interface pair: stop, review, and explain why the extra structure is required.

If the feature needs architecture expansion not anticipated by approved scope: **STOP**, report why / proposed architecture / complexity / simpler alternatives, wait for human approval.

## Size, wrappers, generalization

Avoid giant files **and** micro-files of one-use trivia. Extract when there is a distinct responsibility, reuse, independent tests, or a real readability gain — not to hit a line quota.

Do not build wrapper chains (UI → facade → service → adapter → provider → engine) unless each hop has a concrete reason. Acceptable when security requires it: renderer → typed IPC → service → engine/native tool.

Do not generalize from one case (one renderer, one DB, one PDF engine, one future AI vendor).

## Duplication

Small temporary duplication can beat a wrong abstraction. Rule of Three: first implement clearly; second observe; third consider extracting.

Centralize earlier: security-sensitive logic, money, domain rules, path validation, IPC validation.

## Comments, tests, deps, refactors

Comments explain **why**, not obvious **what**.

Test helpers must not outgrow the code under test. Prefer fixtures and direct tests. Do not add a dependency for trivial helpers; justify each new production dependency in the Phase Report.

No unrelated refactoring during a feature. Record extra debt; do not “clean the whole area” without approval. When replacing an implementation, delete the old one — no zombie dual stacks.

TODO comments must reference a `TD-NNN` / backlog item, or be removed.

## Phase Report

Every implementation Phase Report must include **Complexity Review** (template: `docs/DEVELOPMENT_WORKFLOW.md`). If the implementation could be simpler, simplify **before** asking approval unless the owner accepts the debt.

Before the Phase Report, run `code-review-agent` for an **ANTI-SPAGHETTI REVIEW** (unnecessary abstraction, duplication, dead code, fragmentation, giant units, circular deps, wrappers, unused deps). Do not restyle for preference.

Full workflow: `docs/DEVELOPMENT_WORKFLOW.md`. Architecture: `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`.
